<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/market-failure-externalities">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Markets are extraordinary allocation machines — <strong>when prices tell the truth</strong>.
    The allocation lesson showed how the price mechanism coordinates millions of strangers:
    prices signal scarcity, profits attract resources, competition disciplines sellers. That
    machine has known failure modes, and <strong>market failure is the study of the four big
    ways prices stop telling the truth</strong>: externalities (costs and benefits that escape
    prices), public goods (benefits that cannot be charged for), information asymmetry
    (one side knows too much) and monopoly power (one side sets the price instead of the
    market). Each failure is also the economic justification for some government role —
    which is why this lesson is where economics meets politics: the mixed economy, the
    syllabus's final topic in this unit, is built from exactly these justifications, weighed
    against the government's own failure modes. This page covers the price mechanism's
    conditions, the four failures with their classic evidence, the fix menu (taxes, tradeable
    permits, provision, regulation, property rights), and government failure as the
    honest other side of the ledger.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four classic failures — each a crack in the price signal" widget="equationStep" config={failCfg} />

  <h2>When markets work: the conditions behind the machine</h2>
  <p>
    The invisible hand has fine print. Competitive markets allocate well when
    <strong>prices capture all costs and benefits</strong>, <strong>buyers and sellers are
    many</strong> (no one sets prices), <strong>goods are excludable</strong> (sellers can
    charge users), <strong>information is adequate on both sides</strong>, and
    <strong>property rights exist</strong> (something can be owned and traded). Each
    condition is a niche for a failure: break the first and you get externalities,
    the second monopoly, the third-and-fourth public goods and information
    problems, the fifth the tragedy of the commons. The framing matters
    because it sets the repair philosophy: in each case the machine is not
    broken in general — one <em>price</em> or one <em>contract</em> is missing, and
    the policy question is who can supply it at least cost.
  </p>

  <h2>Externalities: costs and benefits that escape the price</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>externality</strong> exists when a transaction affects third parties who
    neither chose it nor are compensated — so the market price carries only the
    <em>private</em> cost, while the <strong>social cost</strong> (private plus external) is
    what society actually pays. The classic case is the polluting factory:
    its product's price covers labour, steel and capital but not the river,
    the asthma, the fishery. Because the price is a lie — too low — buyers
    buy more than the efficient quantity and too much of society's resources
    flow into polluting production. <strong>A worked example:</strong> suppose a
    chemical costs £300 per tonne to produce privately, but each tonne also
    does £100 of downriver damage. At £300, buyers purchase until the value
    of the last tonne falls to £300 — but society paid £400 for it, so the
    last tonnes destroy value. Output is too high by exactly the tonnes
    valued between £300 and £400. Put a <strong>£100 per tonne tax</strong> on the
    damage (a <strong>Pigouvian tax</strong>, after Pigou's 1920 <em>Economics of
    Welfare</em>) and the private price becomes £400 = social cost: prices
    tell the truth, output falls to the efficient level, and the tax
    revenue can compensate the fishing industry or cut other taxes.
    Externalities run in both directions and both phases: <strong>negative
    consumption</strong> (loud fireworks, cigarettes in shared air),
    <strong>positive production</strong> (a beekeeper's hives polluting — in the
    good sense — a neighbouring orchard), and — the modern giant —
    <strong>positive consumption externalities</strong>: vaccination protects
    others (herd immunity), education raises everyone's wages, so
    under-priced goods are <em>under</em>-consumed and merit subsidies.
    The whole logic of carbon pricing, vaccine subsidies and education
    funding is this one diagram with different numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>The fix menu: taxes, permits, Coase, and property rights</h2>
  <p>
    Economics offers four repairs, each with a personality. <strong>Pigouvian
    taxes and subsidies</strong> price the externality directly — simple, revenue-
    raising, but requiring the damage to be measured (the social cost of
    carbon, the most consequential number in this literature, is estimated
    anywhere from tens to hundreds of dollars per tonne). <strong>Tradeable
    permits</strong> (cap-and-trade, pioneered for US sulphur dioxide in the
    1990 Acid Rain Program, which cut SO₂ emissions faster and cheaper
    than predicted) cap the total and let the market find the cheapest
    reductions — quantity-certain where taxes are price-certain.
    <strong>Regulation</strong> (bans, standards, technology mandates) trades
    efficiency for enforceability and works where measurement is hopeless.
    And <strong>property rights plus bargaining</strong>: Ronald Coase's 1960 insight
    was that if rights are clearly assigned and negotiation is cheap, the
    affected parties can price the externality privately — the factory and
    the fishery settle on a payment — and the efficient outcome arrives
    regardless of who holds the right. The <strong>Coase theorem's</strong> real
    lesson is its failure condition: when transactions costs are high
    (millions of downstream victims), private bargaining collapses and
    government must price or regulate. The <strong>tragedy of the commons</strong>
    (overgrazed pastures, overfished seas, emptied aquifers) is the same
    problem with missing property rights, and the fixes divide exactly:
    privatising the commons, regulating it, or — Elinor Ostrom's Nobel-
    winning fieldwork — the community governing it with its own rules,
    which works more often than the tragedy's name implies.
  </p>

  <h2>Public goods: the free-rider problem</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>public good</strong> is <em>non-excludable</em> (you cannot stop
    non-payers benefiting) and <em>non-rival</em> (my use does not diminish
    yours): national defence, street lighting, flood defences, clean air,
    basic research. The market's problem is structural, not moral: since
    the good cannot be sold per-user, each person rationally
    <strong>free-rides</strong> — enjoys the benefit whether or not they pay — so
    voluntary funding under-supplies or kills the good. <strong>A worked
    example:</strong> street lighting costing £10,000 that delivers £12,000 of
    benefit across a street of 100 houses (£120 each) will not sell
    door-to-door — each house reasons its £100 contribution alone won't
    save the project, so why pay? — and a project worth £2,000 to society
    never happens. Taxation solves the collection problem by making
    contribution non-voluntary, which is why the purest public goods are
    almost everywhere government-supplied and tax-funded. Two
    refinements keep the category honest. First, the name is a technical
    term, not a funding label: a "public good" in the economic sense may
    be privately supplied where exclusion can be engineered (broadcast
    television was funded by adverts — excludability re-created by
    bundling; lighthouses, Coase showed, were often privately built in
    England by charging <em>ports</em> rather than ships — exclusion possible
    at the harbour). Second, goods can be partially public: crowded
    beaches and congested roads are rival but non-excludable
    (<strong>commons</strong>), subscription TV is excludable but non-rival
    (<strong>club goods</strong>) — the 2×2 of excludability and rivalry is the
    real map, and policy differs by quadrant.
  </p>

  <h2>Information asymmetry: the market for lemons</h2>
  <p>
    Markets presume both sides know what is being traded. <strong>Adverse
    selection</strong> is what happens when they do not. George Akerlof's
    "The Market for Lemons" (1970) used used cars: sellers know whether
    their car is a peach or a lemon, buyers cannot tell, so buyers offer
    an average price — which peach-owners refuse and lemon-owners accept,
    worsening the pool's average, lowering the offered price further,
    until only lemons trade. The market does not misallocate slightly;
    it <strong>unravels</strong>. The same logic runs through insurance (the
    sickest applicants are the keenest buyers), credit and labour
    markets, and the repairs are everywhere once you can see them:
    <strong>warranties and brand reputations</strong> (signals too expensive
    for lemons to fake), <strong>certification and inspection</strong> (the MOT
    test, food labelling, ratings agencies), <strong>signalling</strong>
    (education as an expensive-to-fake trait, in Michael Spence's
    model), and <strong>screening</strong> (insurance deductibles that make
    risky customers reveal themselves). A second failure,
    <strong>moral hazard</strong>, arrives <em>after</em> the contract: insured
    drivers brake later, bailed-out banks take bigger risks —
    asymmetry of <em>action</em> rather than <em>information</em>, managed
    with deductibles, co-pays and skin in the game.
  </p>

  <h2>Monopoly power: when the market has one voice</h2>
  <p>
    With many small firms, price is discovered by competition. With a
    <strong>monopoly</strong>, the firm chooses price, and it chooses above
    marginal cost — producing less, charging more, and creating
    <strong>deadweight loss</strong>: transactions that buyers value above
    cost but that never happen because the monopolist protects its
    margin. Sometimes the monopoly is protected by law (patents —
    deliberately bought inefficiency, to fund invention); sometimes by
    scale: a <strong>natural monopoly</strong> exists where one network is
    cheapest — water pipes, rail track, the electricity grid — and
    duplicating the infrastructure to create competition would be
    absurd. The standard response is not competition but
    <strong>regulation</strong> (price caps, rate-of-return rules) or public
    ownership, plus the modern trick of <strong>structural separation</strong>:
    regulate the pipe, compete the services on top (the model behind
    Britain's rail track and telecoms wholesale). Antitrust law
    (Sherman Act 1890 onwards) handles monopolies that formed by
    conduct rather than by cables, and the current frontier is
    platform economics — network effects building winner-take-most
    markets where the product is free and the price signal hides
    in attention and data.
  </p>

  <h2>Government failure: the honest other side</h2>
  <p>
    Every fix above hands power to an institution with its own incentives.
    <strong>Regulatory capture</strong> (the regulated industry writing the rules —
    through lobbying, expertise and revolving doors), <strong>information
    problems</strong> (the regulator knows less than the firms), <strong>blunt
    instruments</strong> (a national standard where local knowledge would
    serve), <strong>rent-seeking</strong> (resources spent competing for political
    favours rather than producing), and plain electoral short-termism
    are all documented and recurring. The mature position — the syllabus's
    mixed-economy endpoint — is <strong>comparative</strong>: not "markets fail,
    therefore government" nor "government fails, therefore markets", but
    which institution, with its particular failure mode, handles
    <em>this</em> problem least badly? Carbon pricing beat command-and-
    control for power-plant sulphur; communities beat both privatisation
    and nationalisation for some fisheries; regulation beat bargaining
    for air safety. The failures and the fixes are the same ledger,
    read from two sides.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Measuring the damage.</strong> The entire Pigouvian programme lives or
    dies on measurement. Valuing a statistical life, a tonne of carbon or a
    decibel of noise forces economics into moral philosophy — and the social
    cost of carbon estimates used by governments (from under $20 to over
    $190 per tonne depending on discount rate and climate sensitivity)
    differ by an order of magnitude, which is an honest map of the
    uncertainty, not a scandal.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Behavioural wrinkle.</strong> Real consumers do not always respond to
    prices as the model assumes: sugar taxes work partly through
    <strong>salience</strong> and default effects, pension auto-enrolment works
    through inertia, and congestion charges work better when framed as
    charges than as taxes. The nudge programme (Thaler and Sunstein)
    adds a fifth instrument — choice architecture — to the fix menu,
    potent but paternalism-contested.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How do you price a global externality across
    borders with no world government (the Paris Agreement's architecture
    is one attempt)? Can platforms' data externalities — your attention
    subsidised by your data — be internalised at all? And as climate
    damages compound, does the "price it" toolbox still suffice, or do
    catastrophic risks demand quantity limits and technology mandates?
    The theory is settled; the calibration is politics.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Pigou, The Economics of Welfare (1920); Coase, "The Problem of Social Cost" (1960) and "The Lighthouse in Economics" (1974); Akerlof, "The Market for Lemons" (1970); Ostrom, Governing the Commons (1990); OpenStax, Principles of Economics.</p>

